# runstate

**The stateful agent run, as a primitive.** runstate is an agent orchestration
runtime where every unit of work is a *run*: budgeted, resumable, and
receipted. You give it an objective and a budget; it drives a model through a
recorded reasoning loop, enforces step/cost/time budgets, persists every step
to a local SQLite ledger, and hands you an itemized receipt when it finishes.

Built for the Open Agent Hackathon 2026 (Track 03, Reasoning Architecture).

```
runstate run "Summarize the quarterly report" --max-steps 10 --max-cost 0.50
```

## Why it exists

Agent frameworks give you prompts and tool loops. They do not give you the
operational primitives that make agents safe to run unattended: a hard budget
that stops the loop, a durable record of what happened and what it cost, and
the ability to resume after a crash. runstate makes the *run* a first-class
object with all three.

## Install

Requires Python 3.10+.

```bash
pip install .
```

Or run the installer, which installs and then runs the doctor:

```bash
./install.sh
```

(`pip install runstate` will work once the package is published to PyPI.)

`runstate doctor` checks the Python version, home directory writability, ledger
writability, the runtime contract, provider configuration, and the MCP
protocol server module, then prints a checklist. It exits 0 when every hard
check passes. A missing provider is a warning, not a failure: the CLI works
without one, only `runstate run` needs it.

## 5-minute quickstart

```bash
# 1. point runstate at a model provider
export RUNSTATE_PROVIDER=nebius
export NEBIUS_API_KEY="<redacted>"
# (or RUNSTATE_PROVIDER=openai with OPENAI_API_KEY)

# 2. start a run: objective plus a hard budget
runstate run "Summarize the quarterly report" --max-steps 10 --max-cost 0.50
```

Real output (walkthrough used a local deterministic stand-in provider; with a
real model the shape is identical, only the words differ):

```
started run run-4702142055de87ae
  step 1 [assistant] $0.0001  The quarterly report shows revenue up 12% with churn down to 3%.
  step 2 [assistant] $0.0001  FINAL: Revenue up 12%, churn down to 3%. Summary complete.

Run       run-4702142055de87ae
Status    success
Objective Summarize the quarterly report
Steps     2 (max 10)
Cost      $0.0001 / $0.5000 budget
Tokens    180 prompt + 32 completion
Outcome   Revenue up 12%, churn down to 3%. Summary complete.
```

```bash
# 3. inspect it later
runstate status run-4702142055de87ae

# 4. get the itemized receipt
runstate receipt run-4702142055de87ae
```

```
Receipt for run run-4702142055de87ae
Status: success

seq  kind        tokens      cost  content
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1  assistant      138   $0.0001  The quarterly report shows revenue up 12% wi
  2  assistant       74   $0.0001  FINAL: Revenue up 12%, churn down to 3%. Sum
  3  outcome          0   $0.0000  Revenue up 12%, churn down to 3%. Summary co
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Total steps:  2
Total tokens: 180 prompt + 32 completion
Total cost:   $0.0001 (budget $0.5000)
Wall time:    0.0s (budget 600s)
```

```bash
# 5. list and manage runs
runstate list
runstate list --status success
runstate cancel run-4702142055de87ae
```

`status` and `receipt` accept `--json` for machine-readable output. Full
walkthrough: [QUICKSTART.md](QUICKSTART.md).

## How it works

```
                        +---------------------+
                        |     runstate CLI    |  run / status / receipt /
                        |   (runstate.cli)    |  cancel / list / doctor
                        +----------+----------+
                                   | drives
                        +----------v----------+
                        |   runner loop       |  check_budget -> provider.complete
                        | (runstate.runner)   |  -> append_step -> usage.add_step
                        +----------+----------+
                                   | persists
              +--------------------+--------------------+
              |                                         |
   +----------v----------+                   +----------v----------+
   |  runtime            |                   |  MCP protocol       |
   |  runstate.runtime   |                   |  server             |
   |  Run / Budget /     |                   |  runstate.protocol  |
   |  Usage / ledger     |                   |  .server            |
   |  (SQLite)           |                   |  run_start/run_step |
   +----------+----------+                   |  /run_status/...    |
              |                              +----------+----------+
   +----------v----------+                              |
   |  provider router    |                   +----------v----------+
   |  nebius | openai    |                   |  sponsor adapters   |
   |  (OpenAI-compatible)|                   |  Zetaris / Meterless|
   +---------------------+                   +---------------------+
```

The run primitive: `create_run(objective, budget)` returns a pending run.
The runner loop drives it to a terminal status (`success`, `exhausted`,
`failed`, `cancelled`), saving after every step, so a crashed run can be
resumed. Budgets cap steps, wall-clock seconds, and USD cost; the ledger is an
append-only SQLite record of every step and its cost; the receipt is derived
from the ledger, never from memory. The MCP server exposes the same primitive
to agents: one tool call advances a stateful, budgeted run. Sponsor adapters
(Zetaris, Meterless) plug into the run lifecycle. Details:
[ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md).

## CLI reference

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `runstate run "objective" [--max-steps N] [--max-cost USD] [--provider NAME] [--timeout S]` | Start a run and drive it to completion. Exit 0 on success, 1 on exhausted/failed, 2 on bad input or missing provider. |
| `runstate status <run-id> [--json]` | Show a run's state, budget, usage, and outcome. |
| `runstate receipt <run-id> [--json]` | Itemized per-step cost breakdown plus totals. |
| `runstate cancel <run-id>` | Cancel a pending/running run (idempotent on terminal runs). |
| `runstate list [--status STATUS] [--json]` | List runs, newest first. |
| `runstate doctor` | Environment checklist; exit 0 when all hard checks pass. |

The model signals completion by starting its final message with `FINAL:`
followed by a summary. That summary is stored as the run's outcome step in
the ledger.

## Environment variables

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `RUNSTATE_PROVIDER` | (unset) | `nebius` or `openai`; selects the model provider |
| `NEBIUS_API_KEY` | (unset) | API key for the Nebius provider |
| `OPENAI_API_KEY` | (unset) | API key for the OpenAI provider |
| `RUNSTATE_MODEL` | provider default | Override the model id (default: `nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Nano-30B-A3B` on Nebius, `gpt-4o-mini` on OpenAI) |
| `RUNSTATE_DB_PATH` | `~/.runstate/ledger.db` | SQLite ledger location |
| `RUNSTATE_HOME` | `~/.runstate` | Writable home directory for runstate state |
| `RUNSTATE_DEFAULT_MAX_STEPS` | `50` | Default step budget for `runstate run` |
| `RUNSTATE_DEFAULT_MAX_COST_USD` | `1.00` | Default cost budget (USD) for `runstate run` |
| `RUNSTATE_RUN_TIMEOUT_S` | `300` | Seconds `runstate run` waits before leaving the run resumable |
| `RUNSTATE_HOST` / `RUNSTATE_PORT` | `127.0.0.1` / `8899` | Bind address for the MCP protocol server |
| `RUNSTATE_PROTOCOL_DIR` | (unset) | Dev override: directory containing the `protocol/` package |

## MCP server

```bash
python -m runstate.protocol.server
```

Serves the runstate protocol over Streamable HTTP (default
`http://127.0.0.1:8899`). Tools: `run_start`, `run_step`, `run_status`,
`run_cancel`, `run_receipt`, plus the `zetaris_query` sponsor adapter. Any MCP
client can drive a stateful, budgeted run with one tool call per step.

## Development

```bash
python -m pytest        # full suite: CLI, runner, e2e against the real runtime
```

Cursor/VS Code: the repo ships `.cursor/rules/` (layout and conventions) and
`.vscode/settings.json`, so it opens cleanly with the Cursor editor required
by the hackathon build rules.

